# **Four things at once: light through the crest, the bed through the column,
# foam, and spray — from a camera above the water, on a sea that does not
# repeat.**
#
#     loom render assets/test/lucent.loom --sim 300 --size 1920x1080 --out /tmp/lucent.png
#     loom-scene lucent --play
#
# Every other sea in this repository shows one of those. `ocean_tropical` is the
# colour, `whitecaps` the foam, `spindrift` the spray, `shore` the shallow band.
# This one has to hold all four in one frame, and they do not want the same
# scene. **It is also the scene the library iterates on**: it is in `SCENES`, it
# is the `water_glow` row of `ABLATE`, and `loom_cli`'s
# `lucent_breaks_and_throws` measures its sea off the screen. It is deliberately
# **not** in `GOLDEN` — every rendering path in it is covered by a reference
# elsewhere, and a reference image of an aesthetic deliverable is the human's to
# bless.
#
# # The sea is an FFT dual spectrum, and that is what stopped it corrugating
#
# **It used to be three authored Gerstner waves and it looked like three
# authored Gerstner waves.** Long-crested, evenly spaced, the same wave repeated
# across the mid-field in rows: the corrugation this branch was opened to fix.
# The fix is `wave_model = "spectrum"` — ADR 0076's cascade, three FFT patches
# at 2048 / 256 / 32 m, each periodic on its own scale, so nothing in frame
# repeats at a period the eye can find.
#
# ## `wave_model = "spectrum"` on its own is not the fix, and that is measured
#
# A single wind sea is a **flatter** picture than the Gerstner triple it
# replaces, not a rougher one, because a fetch-limited spectrum puts its energy
# in long low-steepness waves. The control, same wind, same everything else,
# one 20 km fetch and no `swell` table:
#
#     one sea,  fetch 20 000       Hs 2.534 m   breaking  2.427%   painted  3.827%   903 droplets
#     two seas, 3 500 + 4 200      Hs 1.282 m   breaking  7.824%   painted 10.598%  2583 droplets
#
# **The taller sea is the one that does not break.** Splitting the fetch halves
# the peak wavelength of each half, and `mu_max` is a compression — it scales
# with `k`, not with height — so two short seas crossing at 70° fold three times
# as much surface as one long one carrying twice the energy. That is the same
# argument the three-wave set used to make with hand-authored `steepness`
# values, now made by the fetch law in `loom_water::spectrum`.
#
# ## Short fetches are what keep it inside the depth limit
#
# `Hs` is `0.0016·U10·√(F/g)` and `mu_max` is very nearly blind to `F` at a fixed
# wind. Measured on this scene, at U10 = 35 m/s throughout:
#
#     fetches (m)     Hs       H (crest-trough)   breaking   painted   droplets
#      3 500 + 4 200  1.282 m   2.246 m            7.824%    10.598%     2583
#      4 000 + 5 000  1.399 m   2.535 m            8.044%    10.787%     2520
#      9 000 + 11 000 2.233 m   3.934 m            8.389%    11.285%     2520
#
# **The fetch is a height knob with the breaking left alone**, which is exactly
# the freedom this scene needs: it wants a sea that breaks over three metres of
# water. The shipped pair is the shortest of the three, because it is the one
# that fits under Munk's breaker index — see below.
#
# ## The TOML trap this scene was written into once
#
# `spray`, `fetch` and every other `WaterBody` scalar **must appear before the
# first sub-table header**. Written after `[node.components.WaterBody.swell]`,
# `spray = 8.0` parses as `swell.spray`, the body authors no spray at all,
# `Ocean::keep`'s ring never runs, and the scene renders `particles: 0` with no
# error anywhere. It is silent and it costs the whole fourth subject.
#
# # The tension, and how it is settled
#
# **Seeing the bed wants shallow, clear, calm water. Foam and spray want a
# steep, energetic sea. Backlighting wants a low sun; seeing *into* water wants
# a high one.** Three fights, and the settlement is different in each.
#
# **Steep and shallow is not a contradiction here, and the reason is `k`.**
# `mu_max` — the quantity the whitecaps, the spray and the breaking test all key
# on — is the largest eigenvalue of `Σ steepness·k·A·(d ⊗ d)·sin φ`, so it is
# governed by `k·A`: **wave height over wavelength, not wave height.** A young
# sea under a hard wind has large `k`, so it reaches the same fold as a tall
# swell at a fifth of the height. Measured on the 251×251 grid over 160 m at
# `--sim 300` (`loom_cli`'s `lucent_breaks_and_throws`):
#
#     mu_max mean 0.1190   past 0.22 (foam onset) 24.54%   past 0.33 (breaking) 7.82%
#     crest +1.118 m   trough -1.128 m   H 2.246 m   Hs 1.282 m
#
# — where `WATER_FOAM_BREAK` and `SPRAY_BREAK` are both 0.33 — while the sea is
# only 2.25 m from trough to crest. That is what lets a breaking sea sit over
# three metres of water.
#
# **`Wind.speed` stopped being decoration and the number had to move: 11 → 38.53
# m/s.** On the Gerstner sea the wind drove the Cox–Munk capillary slope and the
# foam's wander and *nothing else* — the three waves were authored beside it and
# took nothing from it. Under `spectrum` the wind **is** the sea, so it is now
# the only knob on how much of this surface breaks, and it is authored at what
# the picture needs rather than at a mood.
#
# **38.5321 is a measured number — do not tidy it to 38.5 or to 40.**
# `Wind.speed` is the free-stream value the height profile saturates toward and
# at the meteorological standard 10 m the profile is 0.90833 of it, so this is
# `U10 = 34.999985` through `Wind::mean_speed_at(10.0)`. The same arithmetic
# `ocean_fft.loom` documents at its own `19.8165`.
#
# **U10 = 35 m/s is hurricane force and this scene is a stylisation, stated
# rather than hidden.** Monahan's relation is `W = 3.84e-6 · U10^3.41` — the one
# `scene.slang` quotes at 3.2% for `ocean`'s 14 m/s, and it reproduces: 3.11%.
#
#     wave model         U10        painted   Monahan at that U10   ratio
#     gerstner, 3 waves   9.99 m/s   14.10%    0.98%                14.3x
#     spectrum, dual     35.00 m/s   10.60%   70.73%                 0.15x
#
# **The scene went from fourteen times whiter than its own wind to a seventh of
# it.** Neither is a measurement of a real ocean, and the swap is not an
# improvement in realism — it is a change in *what the number is a property
# of*. It was three hand-placed `steepness` values with a wind standing beside
# them; it is now a spectrum, so it moves when the wind moves and there is one
# knob rather than four. A 3.5 km fetch under a 35 m/s wind is a shore-hugging
# sea, which is the sea a 200 m shelf would carry.
#
# **Low sun and a visible bed is settled by looking down rather than by raising
# the sun.** The camera is 4.5 m up and pitched 16° down, so the near half of
# the frame is water seen at 25-45° from the normal — inside Fresnel's
# see-into-it band — while the far half grazes and reads as sky and crest. The
# sun sits at **6.9° of elevation, 19° off the camera's bearing**, which puts it
# behind the water the lens is pointed at.
#
# **6.9° is not a mood, it is the condition the subsurface term needs.** After
# the wrap lobe came out (see `scene.slang`'s long comment at the term), the
# backlit-crest path is gated on `saturate(-dot(normal, sunDirection()))` —
# Lambert's cosine on the face the light enters, which for a thin sheet is the
# one *behind* the face you can see. That is non-zero only where the visible
# face is tilted further from the vertical than the sun is above the horizon.
# `ocean_tropical` is the counter-example and it is measured: over a 36-point
# grid at its own render instant its steepest *resolved* face is **9.6°** and its
# sun is 38.3° up, so almost nothing on it can be backlit at all and its
# subsurface term is correctly nearly silent. **Changing the wave model left the
# glow's area alone and made it brighter**: 13.80% of the frame to 13.73%, at a
# mean of 0.60 → 0.84 and a worst channel of 81 → 117. The measurement is in the
# table below; the mechanism is that a young spectrum sea presents more steep
# faces to a 6.9° sun, which is inference and not something measured here.
#
# # The four things, each a number
#
# Measured at `--sim 300`, 1280x720, at this camera. `LOOM_ABLATE` switches an
# effect off; the fraction is `loom compare` at **zero** tolerance against the
# same frame with it on. The right-hand column is the same measurement on the
# three-wave Gerstner sea this replaced, at the same commit.
#
#                                    FFT dual spectrum        Gerstner triple
#     the bed through the water   69.89%  mean 15.12  w162   70.94%  14.42  145
#     subsurface glow             13.73%  mean  0.84  w117   13.80%   0.60   81
#     foam                        17.68%  mean  2.36  w188   25.13%   3.41  129
#     spray                        0.50%  mean  0.14  w178    0.56%   0.17  172
#     droplets in the air          2,583                      3,339
#
# **Foam is the subject that got weaker and it is the one to watch.** 25.13% to
# 17.68% of the frame, and 14.10% to 10.60% of the surface painted white. It is
# not a wiring failure — the ablation still moves 17.7% of the frame and the CPU
# grid still finds 7.8% of the sea past `WATER_FOAM_BREAK` — it is that an
# honest spectrum does not reach 0.33 over as much of its surface as three waves
# authored at `steepness` 0.8 did. Every other subject held or improved: the
# glow is unchanged in area and **40% brighter in the mean**, the bed is
# unchanged, and spray lost 23% of its droplets while gaining worst-channel
# contrast.
#
# **Water is 77.41% of this frame** (measured the same way, against a render
# with the `Water` node deleted), so the bed is doing visible work over **90.3%
# of the water**. Spray's fraction is small and its worst channel the largest in
# the table, which is what a scatter of bright sub-metre droplets looks like to
# a pixel diff: **2,583 droplets are in the air at this instant**, and the same
# scene with `spray = 0.0` renders `particles: 0`.
#
# The bed's number and the spray's are against edited copies of this scene rather
# than against ablations, because neither is a switchable effect: `behind * T` is
# the refraction path itself, and spray is a scene-authored population.
#
# # What this scene is NOT showing, and why
#
# **It has no surf, and it must not be read as having any.** `SEA-REBUILD.md`
# §4.1's depth-limited breaking limiter and §5's curl sheet are both unbuilt. So:
#
# - **Nothing here enforces the depth limit; the scene is authored to respect
#   it.** Munk's breaker index puts a wave's height at breaking at about
#   `0.78 · h`. A spectrum sea has no deterministic all-in-phase ceiling to
#   check that against, so what is checked is what the sea actually puts up:
#   over the 251×251 grid at the render instant the crest is **+1.118 m**, the
#   trough **-1.128 m**, so `H = 2.246 m` over `h = 3.2 m` and **`H/h = 0.702`**,
#   inside 0.78. `Hs` is 1.282 m, `Hs/h = 0.40`. Those numbers are checked, not
#   hoped, and the test prints them: **raising either `fetch` is what breaks
#   this**, because the fetch law is where the height lives. The 9 000 + 11 000
#   row in the table above reaches `H/h = 1.23` and is outside the index.
# - **`attenuation_depth` reads 9.44 m and it is inert.** A `spectrum` body has
#   it *derived* rather than defaulted — `bands` sets it to half the longest
#   band — but `sample_cascade` does not shoal at all: a cascade is a periodic
#   deep-water tile and cannot feel a bed (ADR 0076). So what is drawn is still
#   a deep-water sea with a bed painted underneath it, exactly as before; only
#   the number the CLI reports changed. A scene that wants real shallows wants
#   the Gerstner model until SEA-REBUILD §3's bake exists.
# - **A crest here goes white and throws droplets; it never plunges.** There is
#   no curl, no lip, no barrel and no broken-water sheet after the break.
#
# # The water
#
# **Jerlov II**, the middle open-ocean row of `WaterOptics`'s table: `K_d` =
# 0.3712 / 0.0760 / 0.0383 and `b_b` = 0.00528 / 0.00727 / 0.01091, `G/B` 0.394.
# Jerlov III (`ocean_tropical`'s row) was rendered beside it and is more
# turquoise but hazes the bed out; Jerlov IB shows the bed most sharply and goes
# navy in the troughs. II is where both halves are legible at once, which is the
# whole request.
#
# **3.2 m of water, and the shelf is 200 m square.** Its far edge is a
# discontinuity — the same trap `shore.loom` and `ocean_tropical.loom` name — and
# `fog_density = 0.008` is what hides it. The bed is bright carbonate sand,
# nearly neutral, so the colour in the frame is the water's.
#
# **Deepening the bed was measured and rejected.** A 4.5 m shelf costs the bed
# 70.36% of the frame at mean 15.58 → 69.22% at 12.55, and a 5.05 m one → 68.86%
# at 11.58: the bed stays on nearly as many pixels and gets steadily dimmer on
# each of them, which is the wrong half of the trade. It would have bought room
# for a taller sea; short fetches buy the same breaking without it.
#
# # The camera, and the two alternatives
#
# Swept at 1280x720 and kept in `.superpowers/sdd/sea-showcase/`:
#
#     waterline   eye 2.2 m, pitch  -8°   one wave face fills the frame; most
#                                         drama, least bed
#     shelf       eye 4.5 m, pitch -16°   this one — bed and crests both legible
#     overlook    eye 8.0 m, pitch -26°   the whole wave field and the most bed
#
# The overlook was rejected on the Gerstner sea because from up there it read as
# corrugation. That specific objection is gone with the wave model and the sweep
# is owed a re-run; the shelf camera is kept because it is the one that puts the
# bed and the backlit crests in one frame, which was always the stronger reason.
#
# Sun elevation was swept at 6.9 / 10.1 / 19.9°: at 20° the glow washes flat over
# the whole sea and the wave structure goes with it, which is the failure mode
# the subsurface term's own comment records. **That sweep was also taken on the
# Gerstner sea.** Its conclusion is a property of the shading term rather than of
# the surface, so it is expected to survive, but it has not been re-run and
# should not be quoted as if it had.
#
# **No boat.** `ocean_fft_boat.loom` is the scene with scale in it; a hull here
# covers the water that is the subject. The `Marker` is `spindrift.loom`'s
# device instead — one 3.6 m pile, 20 cm square, off to the left — because a
# still of open water has no scale in it and a droplet could be any size at all.

[scene]
format = 1
id = "6b2e4f19-30ad-4c7e-b158-9e0d7a5c2f84"

[[node]]
name = "Sea"

  # A low sun, 19° off the lens's bearing rather than straight down it, so the
  # glitter path is beside the subject instead of on top of it. The sky is
  # deliberately not a saturated blue: whatever the sea reflects should be near
  # neutral, or a blue sky makes a blue sea for a reason that has nothing to do
  # with the water.
  [node.components.Environment]
  sun_direction = [0.33, 0.12, -0.936]
  sun_strength = 1.8
  sun_color = [1.0, 0.94, 0.84]
  ambient = 0.34
  sky_zenith = [0.10, 0.26, 0.56]
  sky_horizon = [0.58, 0.66, 0.74]
  fog_density = 0.008
  fog_falloff = 0.05

  # **This is the sea, not a mood.** Under `wave_model = "spectrum"` the wind
  # sea below is built from this speed and the body's `fetch`, so `speed` is the
  # only knob on how much of this surface breaks — see the header for the wind
  # the picture asked for and what it costs in honesty.
  #
  # **`speed` is free-stream, not U10, and 38.5321 is measured — do not tidy
  # it.** `loom_field::wind` warns that the authored field is the value the
  # height profile saturates toward, and at the meteorological standard 10 m
  # the profile is 0.90833 of it. Through `Wind::mean_speed_at(10.0)`:
  #
  #     speed 38.50    -> U10 34.970829
  #     speed 38.5321  -> U10 34.999985   <- this
  #     speed 40.00    -> U10 36.333332
  #
  # It also still does the two things it did before the wave model changed: it
  # drives the Cox–Munk capillary slope the shading normal carries
  # (`WATER_DETAIL_*`), which is what softens the backlit cosine at the
  # terminator, and it sets the foam's own wander.
  [node.components.Wind]
  direction_degrees = 190.0
  speed = 38.5321
  gustiness = 1.0
  turbulence = 0.9

# **The shelf: bright sand, 3.2 m down, 200 m square.** One box op at a
# one-metre voxel — a flat bed needs nothing finer and the cost is cubic in the
# resolution. The volume's near corner is its node, so this spans x −90..90 and
# z −130..50; the camera stands at z = 16 and looks toward −Z, so there are
# 146 m of shelf in front of it.
[[node]]
name = "Shelf"
parent = "Sea"
transform = { pos = [-90.0, -24.0, -130.0] }

  [node.components.VoxelVolume]
  voxel_size = 1.0
  chunks = [6, 1, 6]

  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "box"
  center = [96.0, 10.8, 96.0]
  half_extents = [98.0, 10.0, 98.0]
  mode = "union"

  # Carbonate sand: bright and nearly neutral. A yellow bed would put its own
  # hue in the frame, and the hue here is supposed to be the water's.
  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.84, 0.80, 0.70]
  roughness = 0.92

[[node]]
name = "Water"
parent = "Sea"

  # **Every scalar here has to sit above the first sub-table header.** TOML
  # gives a bare key to whatever table was opened last, so a `spray` written
  # after `[...WaterBody.swell]` becomes `swell.spray`, is dropped, and the
  # scene loses its whole spray population with no error. See the header.
  [node.components.WaterBody]
  kind = "ocean"
  # ADR 0076's FFT cascade — 2048 / 256 / 32 m patches, each periodic on its own
  # scale. This is the one field that stops the sea corrugating; every number
  # below only decides which sea it is.
  wave_model = "spectrum"
  surface_height = 0.0
  density = 1025.0
  drag = 2.0
  # How many crests throw, not how hard: a droplet's arc is what the crest that
  # threw it can do. `spindrift.loom`'s value, on a sea with a comparable fold
  # ceiling. Zero is the default and is every sea that predates W5.
  spray = 8.0
  # **The wind sea: 3.5 km of fetch, which is a shore-hugging sea.** Short
  # because `Hs ∝ √F` while `mu_max` barely moves with `F` — so this is the
  # height knob, and it is set at what keeps `H/h` under Munk's 0.78 over a
  # 3.2 m bed. The header has the three-row sweep.
  fetch = 3500.0

    # **The second sea, 70° to port.** One fetch cannot carry both the height
    # and the short steep tail that breaks — measured on this scene, one 20 km
    # sea is *taller* (Hs 2.53 m against 1.28) and breaks over a third as much
    # surface. Splitting it halves each half's peak wavelength, and compression
    # scales with `k`.
    #
    # **A 4.2 km fetch is not a swell in the oceanographic sense and this
    # comment will not pretend it is.** `swell` is what `WaterBody` calls its
    # second spectrum; what is authored here is a second young wind sea off a
    # different reach of the same storm, which is what a crossing sea over a
    # shelf actually is. It carries its own `u10` and takes nothing from the
    # scene's `Wind`.
    [node.components.WaterBody.swell]
    u10 = 21.0
    fetch = 4200.0
    direction = [-0.5, 0.8660254]

    # Jerlov II — a published row, not a colour somebody liked. See the header
    # for why this row and not the two either side of it, and `WaterOptics` for
    # the table and its sources.
    [node.components.WaterBody.optics]
    attenuation = [0.3712, 0.0760, 0.0383]
    backscatter = [0.00528, 0.00727, 0.01091]

# What a droplet off this water looks like. A `ParticleEmitter` under the
# `WaterBody` is the water's splash and never plays where it sits — spray
# borrows its colour and size rather than inventing a second answer to "what
# does this water throw".
[[node]]
name = "Spray"
parent = "Sea/Water"

  [node.components.ParticleEmitter]
  rate = 0.0
  burst = 70
  lifetime = 0.8
  lifetime_jitter = 0.4
  speed = 4.5
  spread_degrees = 55.0
  radius = 0.4
  gravity = -9.0
  drag = 0.5
  turbulence = 0.6
  turbulence_scale = 0.8
  # **A sprite here is a fistful of torn water, not one drop.** 15 cm was the
  # literal reading and it is under two pixels at the range this spray lives at
  # — 15 to 34 m, the far end of `SPRAY_RANGE` — so the crowns read as a scatter
  # of isolated beads and vanished entirely at the 800 px the GIF is judged at.
  # Measured on this frame downscaled to 800: 0.15 -> 0.22 takes legible spray
  # from 0.12% of the frame to 0.37%. 0.30 was rendered too and goes back to
  # reading as blobs — the marker beside it is 20 cm square, and past about one
  # marker width a droplet stops being water in the air.
  size = [0.22, 0.06]
  color_start = [0.95, 0.98, 1.0]
  color_end = [0.70, 0.80, 0.88]
  alpha = [0.9, 0.0]
  seed = 61

# The scale reference. 3.6 m of 20 cm pile, half of it under water — so it also
# carries the waterline band the gap-to-solid foam draws, which nothing else in
# this frame shows.
[[node]]
name = "Marker"
parent = "Sea"
transform = { pos = [-4.2, 0.7, -7.0], scale = [0.20, 3.6, 0.20] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "box" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.16, 0.14, 0.12]
  roughness = 0.85

# See the header's sweep. 4.5 m up and 16° down is the one place both the bed
# and the backlit crests are legible in the same frame.
[[node]]
name = "Camera"
parent = "Sea"
transform = { pos = [0.0, 4.5, 16.0], rot_euler = [-16.0, 0.0, 0.0] }

  [node.components.Camera]
  fov_y_degrees = 55.0
